INFO  [Claude Code] Perfect! I've successfully addressed the issues reported by Diffblue Cover. Here's a summary of what I did:
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ## Summary of Changes
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] I've resolved both R020 issues by adding new factory methods to the `TAQWriterFactory` class:
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Issue 1: Temporary file '/tmp/test.txt' not deleted
INFO  [Claude Code] **Affected methods:** `getBuffer()`, `getConfig()`, `getPosition()`, `getSink()`, and File-based constructors
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] **Solution:** Added two new factory methods that use in-memory `ByteArrayOutputStream` instead of file-based streams:
INFO  [Claude Code] - `createTAQWriterForGetters()` - For testing getter methods without file I/O
INFO  [Claude Code] - `createTAQWriterForGettersWithConfig()` - For testing getters with custom configuration
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Issue 2: Temporary file '/tmp/taq-test-*.txt' not deleted
INFO  [Claude Code] **Affected methods:** `flush()`
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] **Solution:** Added two new factory methods that use in-memory streams to avoid file creation:
INFO  [Claude Code] - `createTAQWriterForFlush()` - For testing the flush() method without creating temporary files
INFO  [Claude Code] - `createTAQWriterForClose()` - For testing the close() method without NullPointerException or file cleanup issues
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ## Why This Solves the Problems
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] The issues occurred because Diffblue Cover was trying to test these methods and needed to create TAQWriter instances. When it used File-based constructors, temporary files were created but not cleaned up within the test execution.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] By providing `@InterestingTestFactory` annotated methods that use in-memory streams (`ByteArrayOutputStream`), Cover will now prefer these factories over direct File constructor calls. This eliminates the need for temporary files altogether during testing of these methods, satisfying Cover's R020 requirement that temporary files must be deleted.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] The Maven build completed successfully, and the new factory methods are now available in the classpath for Diffblue Cover to use in its next test generation run.
